{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat assignments/T-R-hybrid-expert-split.md'","aggregated_output":"# Assignment T-R (campaign c2-resume, host x86-main; owner Claude Opus; worker Codex) — END-TO-END engine candidate\nHypothesis (D014/D019, Strata hit/miss dispatch): for Granite 3.1 1B-A400M (24 MoE layers, 32 experts, top-8), keeping a per-layer HOT expert set on GPU and computing COLD routed experts on the CPU (no H2D upload of misses) beats both layer-granular `--n-cpu-moe` and engine LRU `--moe-cache-mib` at equal GPU MiB (D022 numbers: static n12 1064 MiB 197 t/s; n18 902 MiB 152 t/s). The single-layer harness (cc/hybrid_expert/, D019) showed hybrid dispatch is bitwise-exact per backend and ~33 us per CPU-computed expert. T-O (artifacts/codexO/moe-cache-map.md) established that llama.cpp 31288c9c6 never computes misses on the CPU — so this needs new engine code.\nProposed design (adopt or justify a better one):\n (1) Offline GGUF transform tool `cc/hybrid_split/split_gguf.py` (use gguf-py from ref/llama.cpp/gguf-py, no network): per layer, given a hot set H_l (from cc/moe_static_hot/make_hot_set.py output format), PERMUTE experts so hot ones come first, permuting router rows (ffn_gate_inp) consistently so the model is mathematically identical, and write each expert tensor as two tensors `<name>.hot` [.., |H_l|(+1 null expert if you use the trick below)] and `<name>.cold` [.., 32-|H_l|]. Record the permutation in GGUF metadata. Check the actual Granite MoE tensor names/types in our GGUF (~/models/campaign/gguf/granite-3.1-1b-a400m-instruct-mixE4K.gguf) — read only, don't copy the model into the repo.\n (2) Engine patch (git diff vs 31288c9c6, apply to a scratch copy under artifacts/codexR/tree; never edit ref/): loader accepts the optional split tensors for the Granite MoE arch; build_moe_ffn, when split tensors exist, computes routing as usual, derives ids_hot/ids_cold, runs MUL_MAT_ID on each partition and sums the weighted outputs. Placement is done with the EXISTING `-ot` override (e.g. `-ot '\\.cold=CPU'`), not new placement code.\n (3) Unrouted ids: the CPU path must SKIP them (cost scales with routed cold experts only). ggml-cpu mul_mat_id currently only `assert`s ids in range (ggml-cpu.c ~1655; assert is compiled out in Release) — add explicit `id < 0 -> skip` there. For the GPU hot partition prefer NOT touching CUDA kernels: e.g. append one all-zero \"null expert\" to each .hot tensor and map unrouted slots to it with weight 0 (cheap on GPU); justify. Deriving ids_hot/ids_cold needs integer ops on I32 router ids: find the least invasive way (existing ggml ops, a small new op with CPU+CUDA impl, or computing from the permuted order); state the cost (any device sync / transfer it adds per layer).\n Invariants: default path unchanged when split tensors are absent; routing weights applied exactly once; normalization/scaling identical to the unsplit path; no change to KV/attention; CUDA graph eligibility effect stated.\nTests: CPU-only — the patched tree must compile CPU-only (-DGGML_CUDA=OFF, -j4 max) and you must run a numerical check on CPU: original mixE4K vs split GGUF (hot set from a real trace if available under experiments/routing or cc/routing_trace outputs, else declared synthetic) with llama's CPU backend: identical top-1 tokens for a 64-token greedy continuation of artifacts/traces/in/chat.txt and report max logit difference (use llama-cli/llama-completion or a tiny test binary; keep runs short, -t 4). Pytest for split_gguf.py permutation/router consistency on a tiny synthetic GGUF. Also extend tests/test-backend-ops (or write a focused test) for MUL_MAT_ID with skipped ids so the orchestrator can run it on CUDA.\nAllowed writes: cc/hybrid_split/**, artifacts/codexR/**. Output transformed GGUFs ONLY under artifacts/codexR/models/ (gitignored size ok) — note their sha256.\nDeliver cc/hybrid_split/README.md: integration conditions (shapes, layou